  "text": "Broke, widowed, foreign, picking up other people's leftovers. Three generations later her great-grandson is king. Naomi loses her husband. Then both her sons. Left with two foreign daughters in law in a country that is not hers, she tells them both to go home. Twice. Not cold. Honest. She has nothing to give them. Orpah kisses her and goes. But Ruth clave unto her. And says, \"For whither thou goest, I will go; and where thou lodgest, I will lodge: thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God.\" That is a vow with a curse attached. She means it. They walk into Bethlehem with nothing, right as barley harvest starts. And Ruth goes to work. Gleaning. Following the reapers for what they drop. And the text says her hap was to light on a field belonging to Boaz. Her happenstance. Sure. The book spends two more chapters showing you what that was. Boaz clocks her immediately. Tells his men not to touch her. Then quietly tells them to drop extra grain where she will find it. She goes home with an ephah of barley. An absurdly good day. Chapters later he redeems the family at the town gate. She has a son named Obed. Obed's son is Jesse. Jesse's son is David.",
